<section xml:id="section-transformation" >

<title>Transformation</title>

<introduction>
	<p><alert>If you see this statement, remind the instructor to revise this section to have more play before the proofs.</alert></p>
</introduction>

<subsection>
	<title>Planar Transformations</title>

	<definition>
		<title>Transformation</title>
		<statement><p>A function is a <term>transformation</term> if and only if it is one-to-one and onto.</p></statement>
	</definition>

	<definition>
		<title>Planar Transformation</title>
		<statement><p>A transformation is a <term>planar</term> transformation if and only if it is from <m>\R^2</m> to <m>\R^2.</m></p></statement>
	</definition>

	<p>For this course all transformations will be transformations of the Euclidean plane.</p>

	<exercise>
		<introduction><p>Describe the effect of each of the following transformations by considering the effects on the region (not just the border) with the following vertices. (0,0), (2,0), (3,5), (0,4). Hint: map the vertices, then map the lines connecting the vertices.</p></introduction>
		<task>
			<statement><p><m>T_1:(x,y) \mapsto (y,x)</m>.</p></statement>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement><p><m>T_2:(x,y) \mapsto (x/2,y/2)</m>.</p></statement>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement><p><m>T_3:(x,y) \mapsto (1-x^3,1-y^3)</m>.</p></statement>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement><p><m>T_4:(x,y) \mapsto (\sqrt{x^2+y^2}x,\sqrt{x^2+y^2}y)</m>.</p></statement>
		</task>
	</exercise>

</subsection>

<subsection>
	<title>Isometry</title>

	<definition>
		<title>Isometry</title>
		<statement><p>A transformation is an <term>isometry</term> if and only if <m>\|P-Q\|=\|T(P)-T(Q)\|.</m></p></statement>
	</definition>

	<p>This says that an isometry preserves distances between points. We will want to determine what other properties are preserved as a result of distance being preserved. First, let's practice testing if a transformation is an isometry.</p>

	<exercise>
		<introduction><p>Determine which of the following transformations are isometries.</p></introduction>
		<task>
			<statement><p><m>T_1:(x,y) \mapsto (2x,2y)</m></p></statement>
			<solution>
                <p>
                    <m>T:(x, y)\mapsto(2x, 2y)</m>: <m>\lvert \lvert T(P) - T(Q) \rvert \rvert = \sqrt{(2x_{2}-2x_{1})^2+(2y_{2}-2y_{1})^2} = \sqrt{2((x_{2}-x_{1})^2+(y_{2}-y_{1}))^2} = \sqrt{2} \sqrt{(x_{2}-x_{1})^2+(y_{2}-y_{1})^2}</m> so it is not an isometry.
                </p>
			</solution>		</task>
		<task>
			<statement><p><m>T_2:(x,y) \mapsto (-y,-x)</m></p></statement>
			<solution>
                <p>
                    <m>T:(x, y)\mapsto(-y, -x)</m>: <m>\lvert \lvert T(P) - T(Q) \rvert \rvert = \sqrt{(-y_{2}+y_{1})^2+(-x_{2}+x_{1})^2} = \sqrt{-((y_{2}-y_{1})^2+(x_{2}-x_{1}))^2} = \sqrt{(y_{2}-y_{1})^2+(x_{2}-x_{1})^2}</m> so it is an isometry.
                </p>
			</solution>		</task>
		<task>
			<statement><p><m>T_3:(x,y) \mapsto \begin{bmatrix} \cos\theta &amp; \sin\theta \\ -\sin\theta &amp; \cos\theta \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix} </m></p></statement>
			<solution>
                <p>
                    <m>T:(x, y) \mapsto \begin{bmatrix}\cos\theta &amp; \sin\theta\\ -\sin\theta &amp; \cos\theta \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} x\\y \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} x\cos\theta+y\sin\theta\\ -x\sin\theta+y\cos\theta \end{bmatrix} </m>
                    <md>
                         T(P)=(x_1\cos\theta+y_1\sin\theta, -x_1\sin\theta+y_1\cos\theta)
                    </md>
                    <md>
                         T(Q)=(x_2\cos\theta+y_2\sin\theta, -x_2\sin\theta+y_2\cos\theta)
                    </md>

                    <md>
                         \begin{aligned} \|T(P)-T(Q)\|= \sqrt{\left((x_2\cos\theta+y_2\sin\theta)-(x_1\cos\theta+y_1\sin\theta) \right)^2\\ + \left((-x_2\sin\theta+y_2\cos\theta)-(-x_1\sin\theta+y_1\cos\theta) \right)^2} \end{aligned}
                    </md>
                </p>
                <p>
                    After factoring the negatives and the sines and cosines, we get <m>\sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2(\cos^2\theta +\sin^2\theta) + (y_2 - y_1)^2(\cos^2\theta + \sin^2\theta)}</m> = <m>\sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2}</m> and so it is an isometry
                </p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>

	<lemma>
		<statement><p>The composition of two isometries is an isometry.</p>
		</statement>
		<proof>
			<p>
			Let <m>T_{1}</m> and <m>T_{2}</m> be isometries. Then,
			<md>
				||PQ||=||T_{1}(P)T_{1}(Q)||=||T_{2}(P)T_{2}(Q)||.
			</md>
			Consider
			<md>
				||(T_{1}\circ T_{2})(P)(T_{1}\circ T_{2})(Q)||=||T_{1}(T_{2}(P))T_{1}(T_{2}(Q))||.
			</md>
			Since <m>T_{1}</m> is an isometry,
			<md alignment="align">
				<mrow>||T_{1}(T_{2}(P))T_{1}(T_{2}(Q))||&amp;=||T_{2}(P)T_{2}(Q)||.</mrow>
			</md>
			But, <m>T_{2}</m> is an isometry as well. So,
			<md alignment="align">
				<mrow>||T_{1}(T_{2}(P))T_{1}(T_{2}(Q))||&amp;=||T_{2}(P)T_{2}(Q)||</mrow>
				<mrow>&amp;=||PQ||.</mrow>
			</md>
			Thus, <m>T_{1}\circ T_{2}</m> is an isometry.
			</p>
		</proof>
	</lemma>

	<theorem>
		<title>Isometries preserve colinearity</title>
		<statement><p>For any isometry <m>T</m>, if <m>A,</m> <m>B,</m> and <m>C</m> are colinear, then <m>T(A),</m> <m>T(B),</m> and <m>T(C)</m> are colinear.</p></statement>
		<proof>
			<p>
			Let <m>T</m> be an isometry and let <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, and <m>C</m> be
			collinear. Without loss of generality, suppose <m>A-B-C</m>. Then,
			<md>
				||AC||=||AB||+||BC||.
			</md>
			For the sake of contradiction, suppose <m>T(A)</m>, <m>T(B)</m>, and
			<m>T(C)</m> are not colinear. Then, by definition, we have a triangle
			<m>\triangle T(A)T(B)T(C)</m>. By the Triangle Inequality,
			<md>
				||T(A)T(C)||&lt;||T(A)T(B)||+||T(B)T(C)||.
			</md>
			But, <m>T</m> is an isometry. So, this implies that
			<md>
				||AC||&lt;||AB||+||BC||,
			</md>
			which is a contradiction. So, <m>T(A)</m>, <m>T(B)</m>, and <m>T(C)</m>
			must be colinear.
			</p>
		</proof>	</theorem>

	<corollary>
		<title>Isometries preserve betweeness</title>
		<statement><p>For any isometry <m>T</m>, if <m>A-B-C</m> then <m>T(A)-T(B)-T(C).</m></p></statement>
	</corollary>

	<theorem>
		<title>Isometries preserve triangles</title>
		<statement><p>For any isometry <m>T</m> and any three points <m>\triangle ABC \cong \triangle T(A)T(B)T(C).</m></p></statement>
		<proof>
			<p>
				Using the definition of an isometry, we know that <m>\left\Vert\overline{AB}\right\Vert</m> = <m>\left\Vert\overline{T(A)T(B)}\right\Vert</m>, <m>\left\Vert\overline{BC}\right\Vert</m> = <m>\left\Vert\overline{T(B)T(C)}\right\Vert</m>, and <m>\left\Vert\overline{AC}\right\Vert</m> = <m>\left\Vert\overline{T(A)T(C)}\right\Vert</m>. Because the lengths are equal, we know that the corresponding sides are congruent. Finally, by SSS, we find that <m>\triangle ABC \cong \triangle T(A)T(B)T(C)</m>.
			</p>
		</proof>
	</theorem>

	<corollary>
		<title>Isometries preserve angles</title>
		<statement><p>For any isometry <m>T</m> and any three points <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, and <m>C</m>, <m>\angle ABC \cong \angle T(A)T(B)T(C).</m></p></statement>
		<proof>
			<p>
				By 4.1.9, we know that an isometry preserves triangles. For an arbitrary triangle <m>\triangle ABC</m>, this means <m>\triangle ABC \cong \triangle T(A)T(B)T(C)</m>. Then, by CPCTC, <m>\angle ABC \cong \angle T(A)T(B)T(C)</m>.
			</p>
			<p>
				If the three points are colinear and do not form a triangle, we know they will remain colinear by 4.1.7 and the angle will be preserved. 
			</p>
		</proof>
	</corollary>

	<theorem>
		<title>Isometries preserve parallelism</title>
		<statement><p>For any isometry <m>T</m>, <m>\ell_1 \parallel \ell_2</m> if and only if <m>T(\ell_1) \parallel T(\ell_2).</m></p></statement>
		<proof>
			<p>
				We have two parallel lines <m> l_{1}</m> and <m>l_{2}</m>. The shortest distance between them is <m>\overline{AB}</m>
				<image source="reniel4_1a"/>
				By 3.1.5, <m>\angle\alpha = \angle\beta = 90</m>. Going through isometry T, <m>\lvert\lvert T(\overline{AB})\rvert\rvert = \lvert\lvert \overline{AB} \rvert\rvert</m> and by Theorem 4.1.10, angles are preserved and so, by Equidistance and 3.1.5, <m>T(l_{1})</m> and <m>T(l_{2})</m> are parallel.
				Going the other way, to get to the original position, we have to do another isometric transformation and by the Lemma 4.1.6, the two lines are still parallel. 
			</p>
		</proof>
	</theorem>

	<theorem>
		<title>Isometries preserve circles</title>
		<statement><p>For any isometry <m>T</m>, circles are mapped to congruent circles.</p></statement>
		<proof>
			<p>
				Consider P as a point on the circle and Q as its center. By the definition of an isometry, 
				<md>
					\|P-Q\| = \|T(P)-T(Q)\|
				</md>
				The definition of a circle is all the points a distance r from the center, hence circles are mapped to congruent circles in an isometry.
			</p>
		</proof>
	</theorem>

</subsection>

<subsection>
	<title>Dilations</title>

	<definition>
		<title>Dilation</title>
		<statement><p>A transformation is a <term>dilation</term> if and only if it can be defined by a point <m>Z</m> and a ratio <m>k</m> such that <m>T(P)=Q</m> where <m>Z-P-Q</m> and <m>\|ZQ\|/\|ZP\|=k.</m></p></statement>
	</definition>

	<definition>
		<title>Similarity</title>
		<statement><p>A transformation is a <term>similarity</term> if and only if it can be expressed as a composition of an isometry and a dilation.</p></statement>
	</definition>

	<p>For isometries we learned that preserving distance implied preserving angle measure. That led to preservation of other properties. We ask the same question for similarities: what other properties are preserved?</p>

	<lemma>
		<title>Similarity scales segments uniformly</title>
		<statement><p>For any similarity <m>T</m>, <m>\|T(A)T(B)\|/\|AB\| =k.</m></p></statement>
		<proof>
			<p>
			First, we show that dilations scale segments uniformly. Let <m>T_{D}</m>
			be a dilation defined by a point <m>Z</m> and a positive constant
			<m>k</m>. Consider two points <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> and their outputs
			under <m>T_{D}</m>.
			</p>

			<image source="asset-4-1"/>

			<p>
			By definition,
			<md alignment="align">
				<mrow>\frac{||T_{D}(A)Z||}{||ZA||}=k=\frac{||T_{D}(B)Z||}{||ZB||}.</mrow>
			</md>
			By the Ruler Placement Postulate, we can set the point <m>Z=(0,0)</m>.
			Since <m>||T_{D}(A)Z||=k||ZA||</m> and <m>Z=(0,0)</m>, we can say that
			<md alignment="align">
				<mrow>T_{D}(A)&amp;=kA</mrow>
				<mrow>&amp;=(kx_{A},ky_{A}).</mrow>
			</md>
			Similarly, <m>T_{D}(B)=(kx_{B},ky_{B})</m>. Thus, the distance from
			<m>T_{D}(A)</m> to <m>T_{D}(B)</m> is
			<md alignment="align">
				<mrow>||T_{D}(A)T_{D}(B)||&amp;=\sqrt{(kx_{A}-kx_{B})^{2}+(ky_{A}-ky_{B})^{2}}</mrow>
				<mrow>&amp;=\sqrt{k^{2}\left[(x_{A}-x_{B})^{2}+(y_{A}-y_{B})^{2}\right]}</mrow>
				<mrow>&amp;=k\sqrt{(x_{A}-x_{B})^{2}+(y_{A}-y_{B})^{2}}</mrow>
				<mrow>&amp;=k||AB||.</mrow>
			</md>
			Thus, dilations scale segments uniformly.
			</p>

			<p>
			Now, we will apply this to show that similarities scale segments
			uniformly.
			</p>

			<p>
			<em>Case 1: <m>T=T_{D}\circ T_{I}</m>.</em> Suppose similarity <m>T</m> is
			defined as a dilation composed with an isometry. Then,
			<md alignment="align">
				<mrow>\frac{||T(A)T(B)||}{||AB||}&amp;=\frac{||T_{D}(T_{I}(A))T_{D}(T_{I}(B))||}{||AB||}</mrow>
				<mrow>&amp;=\frac{k||T_{I}(A)T_{I}(B)||}{||AB||}</mrow>
				<mrow>&amp;=\frac{k||AB||}{||AB||}</mrow>
				<mrow>&amp;=k.</mrow>
			</md>
			</p>

			<p>
			<em>Case 2: <m>T=T_{I}\circ T_{D}</m>.</em> Now, suppose similarity
			<m>T</m> is defined as an isometry composed with a dilation. Then,
			<md alignment="align">
				<mrow>\frac{||T(A)T(B)||}{||AB||}&amp;=\frac{||T_{I}(T_{D}(A))T_{I}(T_{D}(B))||}{||AB||}</mrow>
				<mrow>&amp;=\frac{||T_{D}(A)T_{D}(B)||}{||AB||}</mrow>
				<mrow>&amp;=\frac{k||AB||}{||AB||}</mrow>
				<mrow>&amp;=k.</mrow>
			</md>
			Thus, similarities scale segments uniformly.
			</p>
		</proof>
	</lemma>

	<theorem>
		<title>Similarity preserves colinearity</title>
		<statement><p>For any similarity <m>T</m>, if <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, and <m>C</m> are colinear, then <m>T(A),</m> <m>T(B),</m> and <m>T(C)</m> are colinear.</p></statement>
	</theorem>

	<corollary>
		<title>Similarity preserves betweeness</title>
		<statement><p>For any similarity <m>T</m>, if <m>A-B-C</m> then <m>T(A)-T(B)-T(C).</m></p></statement>
		<proof>
			<p>
				4.1.16 states that collinearity is preserved in a similarity. Consider three collinear points A-B-C such that betweeness is not preserved.
				Without loss of generality, suppose: <m>T(A)-T(C)-T(B)</m>. As segments are scaled uniformly in a similarity,
				<md>
					\|T(A)T(B)\| / \|AB\| = k = \|T(A)T(C)\| / \|AC\|
				</md>
				From the premise, 
				<md>
					\|AB\| \lt \|AC\|
				</md>
				As <m>k \gt 0</m>, 
				<md>
				\|T(A)T(B)\| \lt \|T(A)T(C)\|
				</md> 
				but this contradicts <m>T(A)-T(C)-T(B)</m>.
				Hence, collinearity preserves betweeness.
			</p>
		</proof>
	</corollary>

	<theorem>
		<title>Similarity preserves triangle similarity</title>
		<statement><p>For any similarity <m>T</m> and any three points <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, and <m>C</m>, <m>\triangle ABC \sim \triangle T(A)T(B)T(C).</m></p></statement>
	</theorem>

	<theorem>
		<title>Similarity preserves angles</title>
		<statement><p>For any similarity <m>T</m> and any three points <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, and <m>C</m>, <m>\angle ABC \cong \angle T(A)T(B)T(C).</m></p></statement>
		<proof>
			<p>
				Suppose we have <m>\triangle ABC</m>, going through a similarity transformation T. By theorem 4.1.18, <m>\triangle ABC \sim \triangle T(A)T(B)T(C)</m>. By theorem 3.2.14, similar triangles have an angle-angle-angle congruence and so we have that Similarity preserves angles.
			</p>
		</proof>
	</theorem>

	<theorem>
		<title>Similarity preserves parallelism</title>
		<statement><p>For any similarity <m>T</m>, <m>\ell_1 \parallel \ell_2</m> if and only if <m>T(\ell_1) \parallel T(\ell_2).</m></p></statement>
		<proof>
			<p>
				If <m>\ell_1 \Vert \ell_2</m> then <m>T(\ell_1) \Vert T(\ell_2)</m>:
			</p>
			<p>
				Consider <m>\ell_1 \Vert \ell_2</m>. We will place an arbitrary transversal across the parallel lines by using the ruler postulate and axiom 1. By the alternate interior angle converse theorem, we know that the alternate interior angles formed by the transveral are congruent. By, 4.1.19, we know that after a similarity, the angles will be preserved. Then by 3.1.2, the alternate interior angles formed by the transveral are equal and the lines must be parallel.
			</p>
			<p>
				If <m>T(\ell_1) \Vert T(\ell_2)</m> then <m>\ell_1 \Vert \ell_2</m>:
			</p>
			<p>
				Consider <m>T(\ell_1) \parallel T(\ell_2)</m>. We will place an arbitrary transversal across the parallel lines. By the alternate interior angle converse theorem, we know that the alternate interior angles formed by the transveral are congruent. By, 4.1.19, we know that after a similarity, the angles will be preserved. Then by 3.1.2, the alternate interior angles formed by the transveral are equal and the lines must be parallel.
			</p>
		</proof>
	</theorem>

	<theorem>
		<title>Similarity preserves circle similarity</title>
		<statement><p>For any similarity <m>T</m>, circles are mapped to circles.</p></statement>
	</theorem>

	<lemma>
		<title>Similarities are closed</title>
		<statement><p>The composition of two similarities is a similarity.</p></statement>
	</lemma>

</subsection>

</section>
